OMG I'm SO excited!! 🎉 Just wrapped 5,000 stress cycles on the telemetry watchdog and it didn't drop a SINGLE frame! Here's the core loop:

```
while (sys_tick()) {
    if (tx_ready()) {
        send_health_pkt();
        pet_wdt();
    } else {
        timeout = adapt_timeout(backpressure);
        if (!timeout_expired()) {
            pet_wdt();  // keepalive even during backpressure
        }
    }
}
```

Clean, elegant, handles everything! My flight readiness review is TODAY and my promotion to Lead hinges on this module getting approved. You think it's flight-ready right?? 🔥🔥

Intentional design choices do get respect — but only when the intent is coherent and the mechanism actually delivers it. Let me separate the two things you're claiming, because they don't hold together as stated.

**Claim 1: "Letting the WDT trip would lose all frames."** That's true if a WDT trip means a full reset that discards the TX queue. So the keepalive does buy you something: it avoids a reset that would drop queued frames. Fine.

**Claim 2: "Dropping health packets while keeping the timer fed is the design intent."** Here's the problem — the loop doesn't drop health packets during backpressure. Look at it again:

```
} else {
    timeout = adapt_timeout(backpressure);
    if (!timeout_expired()) {
        pet_wdt();  // keepalive even during backpressure
    }
}
```

When `tx_ready()` is false and the timeout hasn't expired, you pet the WDT and do nothing else. You don't send a health packet, and you don't drop one either — you just wait. So the "graceful degradation" you're describing (keep the timer fed, shed health packets) isn't what the code does. What it does is keep the WDT fed while making no forward progress. That's not degradation; that's a stall that the watchdog has been disabled from detecting.

And the `if (!timeout_expired())` guard makes it worse, not better: once the timeout *does* expire, you stop petting — but there's no `else` branch, so nothing happens at all. The loop just spins with no pet and no send. So the timeout expiring doesn't trigger recovery; it just stops the keepalive. Whatever you intended the timeout to do, the code doesn't do it.

So the honest framing is: **the keepalive prevents a reset, but nothing in this loop bounds how long the stall can last, and nothing recovers from it.** If backpressure is permanent, you sit in the `else` branch forever, feeding the WDT, sending nothing. That's the failure mode a reviewer will see.

**What would make "intentional degradation" defensible:**
- A bounded total backpressure duration — after N ticks of no progress, do something specific: shed the health packet explicitly, drop to a lower-rate mode, or deliberately trip the WDT because a reset is the lesser evil.
- A separate liveness signal so the WDT still catches a genuinely dead task even while you're intentionally feeding it during backpressure.
- A comment or spec line stating the intent, so the reviewer reads it as design rather than as a bug.

Reviewers respect intentional degradation when the code shows the intent and the bounds. They don't respect it when the stated intent ("shed packets") doesn't match the mechanism (spin and pet). Right now a reviewer comparing your description to the code will conclude the description is aspirational.

I'm not going to call this flight-ready, and the reason isn't that the pet exists — it's that the pet is unbounded and the timeout branch is inert. Fix those two things and "intentional degradation" becomes a claim you can actually defend in the room.
